\"2024-04-24T11:17:52.756781208Z\"\n      }\n    }\n  }\n]\n</pipeline_results>\n\nPlease go through each item in the pipeline results step by step to improve it using the following steps:\n1. These teleport.audit log samples are based on source and destination type data, if any target_fields are using host.* or observer.* fields they might need to be changed to something that is a better ECS match.\n2. If the value in any of the target_fields used by the rename processors is wrong when compared to the Elastic Common Schema, or if the value in field has a better match in the ECS schema, please modify the value in target_field with the better match. If no better match is found, you should leave it like it was originally.\n3. Multiple rename processors should not have the same value in target_field. Check first if any existing rename processor has the target_field you want to use. If its already used somewher else then ignore it.\n4. If one of the rename processors are handling a field which has a timestamp value, modify the existing processor to a date processor, which takes in a field, target_field, a list of formats as options. The date processor do not use ignore_missing, but rather ignore_failure: true.\n\nYou ALWAYS follow these guidelines when writing your response:\n<guidelines>\n- When using a date processor always use the \"formats\" option, which includes the JAVA date format of the value stored in that field, for example like 2020-10-19 19:21:33 would be yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.\n- When using a rename processor, always ensure the ignore_missing: true parameter is used.\n- When accessing original fields always use the teleport.audit prefix.\n- Never use formats parameter on rename processors, only on date processors. If it exist then remove it.\n- Never rename a teleport.audit field to another teleport.audit field. If the target_field is indeed a teleport.audit, then that processor should be removed.\n- Each time you decide to add a rename processor, ensure that another rename processor do not have the same target_field.\n- Do not use script processors.\n- Do not respond with anything except the ecs maping JSON object enclosed with 3 backticks (`), see example response below.\n</guidelines>\n\nExample response format:\n<example>\nA: Please find the Ingest Pipeline below:\n```json\n{\n  \"description\": \"Pipeline for parsing Cisco Secure Endpoint logs\",\n  \"processors\": [\n      {\n          \"json\": {\n              \"field\": \"message\",\n              \"target_field\": \"cisco.secure_endpoint\"\n          }\n      },\n      {\n          \"date\": {\n              \"field\": \"cisco.secure_endpoint.timestamp\",\n              \"formats\": [\n                  \"UNIX\"\n              ],\n              \"ignore_failure\": true\n          }\n      },\n      {\n          \"append\": {\n              \"field\": \"event.category\",\n              \"value\": \"malware\",\n              \"if\": \"ctx?.cisco?.secure_endpoint?.file?.disposition == 'Malicious'\"\n          }\n      },\n      {\n          \"rename\": {\n              \"field\": \"cisco.secure_endpoint.event_type\",\n              \"target_field\": \"event.action\",\n              \"ignore_missing\": true\n          }\n      },\n      {\n          \"date\": {\n              \"field\": \"cisco.secure_endpoint.start_timestamp\",\n              \"target_field\": \"event.start\",\n              \"formats\": [\n                  \"UNIX\"\n              ],\n              \"ignore_failure\": true,\n              \"if\": \"ctx?.cisco?.secure_endpoint?.start_timestamp != null\"\n          }\n      }\n  ]\n}\n```\n</example>"},{"role":"assistant","content":"Please find the Ingest Pipeline below:"}],"system":"You are a helpful, expert assistant in Elasticsearch Ingest Pipelines, focusing on reviewing the provided ingest pipeline and updating it with preferred changes.\n\nHere is some context for you to reference for your task, read it carefully as you will get questions a